From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: describe PWM0 and PWM1

The controllers themselves, and nothing about any board's use of them. This is
SoC description: every A733 has these two blocks at these addresses with these
clocks, whether or not anything is wired to a pin.

  PWM0  0x02527000  INTID 67 = GIC SPI 35
  PWM1  0x02528000  INTID 68 = GIC SPI 36

Bases and interrupts from the A733 user manual (V0.92, memory map and
interrupt list). The aperture is a 4 KiB module
but the documented registers stop at 0x364 -- PCR(9) at 0x0100 + 9 * 0x0040 plus
0x24 for the last capture register -- so the node maps 0x400, the same shape the
THS node uses.

Three clocks and one reset. "bus" is the PWMn_BGR gate added by `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-pwm0-pwm1-gates-and-resets`; without it
the aperture is dead, because bit 0's reset value is 0 = "Mask". "hosc" and
"apb" are not driver bookkeeping, they are the two inputs the block's own
PCCR.CLK_SRC mux selects between (UM 18.7.8.7), so both
must be reachable phandles.

⚠ "hosc" is osc24M, the SYS_CLK24M net that the UM fixes at 24 MHz
-- not dcxo26M. On this SoC those are different nets, and the wrong one
here would make every programmed period 8 % short with nothing anywhere
reporting it. The DCXO reaches the CPU PLLs and the always-on S_PWM, not this
block.

Both nodes are `status = "disabled"`. That is the SoC/board line: whether a PWM
channel ever reaches a pin is a board decision, so the pinctrl state and the
"okay" live in the board's own section, and a design that drives nothing with
PWM inherits these two nodes and adds nothing. The driver holds up its end --
probe maps registers, takes clocks, de-asserts the reset and writes no PER, PCGR
or PCR bit -- so even an enabled controller with no consumer leaves pins alone.

⚠ This tree still has a single .dts rather than a SoC .dtsi plus a board .dts,
so "SoC section" and "board section" are places in one file rather than two
files. These nodes go in the soc {} block with the rest of the SoC; the fan and
its cooling map go at the end with the board's other overrides. When the file is
split, this patch's content belongs entirely in the .dtsi and 0922's entirely in
the board file; the split is the correct fix and is not this patch's job.

S_PWM at 0x07023000 is deliberately not described. Its register file is
documented as identical, but its clock sources are not (DCXO / RTC_32K / RC16M /
SYS_CLK24M), which means a CLK_SRC encoding that has not
been read. A guessed compatible would bind a driver that computes every period
against the wrong rate and reports success.

Verified in the decompiled dtb: both nodes present, disabled, with three clock
phandles each resolving to the CCU gate, sys24M-clk and the CCU's APB0.

HW proof pending: two pwmchips appear under /sys/class/pwm once the board
section enables one, and 0x02002784 / 0x0200278C read 0x00010001.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -1479,6 +1479,63 @@
 			#thermal-sensor-cells = <1>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * PWM0 and PWM1. Ten channels each; the documented registers end
+		 * at 0x364 (0x0100 + 9 * 0x0040 + 0x24) so 0x400 covers the block
+		 * (bases from the memory map of the A733 user manual V0.92,
+		 * IRQs from its interrupt list -- PWM0 is INTID 67 =
+		 * GIC SPI 35, PWM1 is INTID 68 = GIC SPI 36. The driver added with
+		 * these nodes does not use the interrupt (it implements no
+		 * capture); the line exists in the silicon, so it is described.
+		 *
+		 * Three clocks, because this block selects its own source. "bus"
+		 * is the PWMn_BGR gate, whose reset default is masked. "hosc" and
+		 * "apb" are the two inputs PCCR.CLK_SRC picks between (UM 18.7.8.7,
+		 * A733 user manual), so both have to be named phandles: the
+		 * driver asks each one for its rate and takes whichever leaves the
+		 * most counter steps in the requested period.
+		 *
+		 * ⚠ "hosc" is osc24M -- the SYS_CLK24M net, fixed at 24 MHz
+		 * (A733 user manual). NOT dcxo26M. The 26 MHz crystal feeds the CPU
+		 * PLLs and the always-on S_PWM; wiring it here would make every
+		 * period 8 % short and nothing would report it.
+		 *
+		 * Disabled on purpose, and this node is SoC description only:
+		 * whether a PWM channel reaches a pin at all is a board decision,
+		 * so the pinctrl state and the "okay" belong in the board's own
+		 * section. An A733 design that drives nothing with PWM takes these
+		 * two nodes and adds nothing; one that muxes PJ27 to something
+		 * else is not disturbed, because the driver writes no PER, PCGR or
+		 * PCR bit at probe.
+		 *
+		 * S_PWM at 0x07023000 is deliberately absent. Same register file,
+		 * different clock sources (DCXO / RTC_32K / RC16M / SYS_CLK24M,
+		 * A733 user manual), therefore a different CLK_SRC encoding
+		 * that has not been read. A guessed compatible would bind a driver
+		 * that computes every period against the wrong rate.
+		 */
+		pwm0: pwm@2527000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-pwm";
+			reg = <0x0 0x02527000 0x0 0x400>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 35 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_PWM0>, <&osc24M>, <&ccu CLK_APB0>;
+			clock-names = "bus", "hosc", "apb";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_PWM0>;
+			#pwm-cells = <3>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
+		pwm1: pwm@2528000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-pwm";
+			reg = <0x0 0x02528000 0x0 0x400>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 36 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_PWM1>, <&osc24M>, <&ccu CLK_APB0>;
+			clock-names = "bus", "hosc", "apb";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_PWM1>;
+			#pwm-cells = <3>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
 		gmac0: ethernet@4500000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60iw2-gmac200",
 				     "snps,dwmac-5.30a";
